{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And they embittered him, and multiplied, etc.</b> Meaning: at first they intended to kill him, as it is written (Genesis 37:20) ״לְכוּ וְנַהַרְגֵהוּ״. Or, according to the way of Amram, of blessed memory (Sefer HaYashar, portion of Vayeshev), before they cast him into the pit, when they stripped him, they inflicted upon him many wounds and afflicted him — and corresponding to this it says <b>and they embittered him</b>. Afterward they took counsel against him to cast him into the pit and to sell him to strangers, and corresponding to these divisions it says <b>and multiplied</b> — meaning, they multiplied in afflicting." "<b>And they hated him, the masters of arrows.</b> This is the wife of Potiphar and her companions who were seated at her banquet, who sought from him their arrows — that is, the seed that shoots forth like an arrow." "Or perhaps the verse intends the entirety of what is written upon this particular matter — <b>and they embittered him</b>: they are the aspect of evil that entices one to do evil, and this was for him a cause that his hands would become slack, in that the evil inclination entered into his body, like the deed of Rav Amram Ḥasida (Kiddushin 81a). Now, the aspect of the kelipah is ascribed the name מָרָה (bitterness); therefore it says ״וַיְמָרְרוּהוּ״ — the forces of evil." "<b>And multiplied</b> — through the multiplying of the forces of evil upon him and their forcing him toward transgression, and their prevailing over the one who restrains — <b>and they hated him, the masters of arrows</b>: these are the kelipot that cause a man to err and emit seed in vain that shoots forth like an arrow. And it uses the language of hatred (מַשְׂטֵמָה) because the forces of evil devise evil against holiness, like adversaries (מַשְׂטִימִים) seeking to destroy what is precious. Yet even so, he prevailed like a lion and did not shoot his arrow, but rather <b>his arms were made supple</b>." "And the verse says <b>from the hands of the Mighty One of Jacob</b>, meaning that HaShem was his help, in the manner of the verse's saying (Psalms 37:33) ״HaShem will not leave him in his hand,״ and gave him might against them, for HaShem is the helper against the instigator and the enticer." "And the verse's saying <b>from there the shepherd, the stone of Israel</b> — here is an allusion to (Psalms 118:22) ״אֶבֶן מָאֲסוּ הַבּוֹנִים״, and Yoseif, through the merit of his righteousness in guarding the holy covenant, merited the foundation stone. And this is the sod of (Proverbs 10:25) ״צַּדִּיק יְסוֹד עוֹלָם״, that the righteous man is called ״the shepherd, the stone of Israel.״ Understand this, and from his likeness they granted it to him."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:51:04.978706119Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}